Improving description...
Proposed (11.8s): Use this skill whenever a command-line program stays open and you need to exchange input and output with it across multiple steps — instead of running one-shot commands that exit. This is the right tool the moment a user wants to: keep a session alive and run several commands in it reading output between each; start or reuse a REPL (python, node, irb, psql, sqlite3) and feed it statements one at a time, checking each result before the next; hold an SSH session open across commands; step through a debugger (gdb/pdb); or automate any CLI that prompts and waits. It applies equally whether the process is one you launch now or one already running in a tmux pane (e.g. "psql is open in pane 0:0.1, run these SELECTs"). It works by driving the process through tmux — send keystrokes, poll until the program is actually ready, capture the output back. Do NOT use it for a single command that runs and exits, a file read, or editing config — run those directly with Bash.

